WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.029
Shopify has already documented this problem: an import cannot

00:00:03.029 --> 00:00:06.058
be canceled once it starts, and included blank fields

00:00:06.058 --> 00:00:09.087
can overwrite existing values. Rewind helps you check the

00:00:09.087 --> 00:00:12.116
next import before it runs, and undo exact damaged

00:00:12.116 --> 00:00:14.808
values after a mistake while keeping newer work.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.191
For the next import, bring today's CSV export and

00:00:18.191 --> 00:00:21.062
the update you plan to import. Choose what should

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:23.934
change. Here, I want prices to change, with a

00:00:23.934 --> 00:00:26.805
maximum increase or decrease of twenty-five percent. Payment
statuses

00:00:26.805 --> 00:00:28.400
should stay as they are.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.046
Rewind finds twenty-two ordinary price updates. It holds out

00:00:32.046 --> 00:00:35.173
a price multiplied by one hundred, a blank price,

00:00:35.173 --> 00:00:38.299
and twenty-four unwanted payment changes. The unknown record ID

00:00:38.299 --> 00:00:41.425
stays out too. I can remove any other update

00:00:41.425 --> 00:00:42.120
before approving.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.704
I review the exact twenty-two cells. Approval is tied

00:00:45.704 --> 00:00:48.768
to both files, my chosen rules and the exact

00:00:48.768 --> 00:00:51.831
selection. Changing any of those requires a new plan.

00:00:51.831 --> 00:00:54.895
The import updates existing records by ID and preserves

00:00:54.895 --> 00:00:55.576
other fields.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.621
Rewind builds the approved CSV inside gVisor on Vultr,

00:00:59.621 --> 00:01:03.163
then executes that import against a disposable copy. Fresh

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:06.704
workers inspect both outputs. The controller independently checks
every

00:01:06.704 --> 00:01:10.245
approved and protected value. This recurring check makes no

00:01:10.245 --> 00:01:11.032
model calls.

00:01:11.532 --> 00:01:14.519
The import is checked and ready. All twenty-four records

00:01:14.519 --> 00:01:17.506
and their payment statuses remain. I download the approved

00:01:17.506 --> 00:01:20.493
import and resulting export, with a complete change list.

00:01:20.493 --> 00:01:23.480
If the business data changes before importing, I check

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.140
again against a fresh export.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:28.654
When an import has already gone wrong, I bring

00:01:28.654 --> 00:01:31.656
three versions: the last good copy, a checkpoint just

00:01:31.656 --> 00:01:34.658
after the mistake, and today's file. The checkpoint helps

00:01:34.658 --> 00:01:37.659
identify the later edits that should stay. CSV and

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:38.660
SQLite are supported.

00:01:39.172 --> 00:01:42.474
I describe the mistake: prices were multiplied by one

00:01:42.474 --> 00:01:45.775
hundred. Vultr Serverless Inference writes diagnostic code that
actually

00:01:45.775 --> 00:01:49.077
compares the copies in its own gVisor environment. The

00:01:49.077 --> 00:01:52.379
controller checks the proposed repair against separate trusted
inspections,

00:01:52.379 --> 00:01:54.580
rather than trusting the agent's description.

00:01:55.092 --> 00:01:58.473
The agent proposes restoring one hundred seventeen prices. Three

00:01:58.473 --> 00:02:01.853
prices changed again afterward, so they are protected. I

00:02:01.853 --> 00:02:05.234
review and approve the exact repair. A whole-backup replacement

00:02:05.234 --> 00:02:08.615
would lose newer invoices and updates. Rewind keeps today's

00:02:08.615 --> 00:02:11.244
records and every value outside my approval.

00:02:11.744 --> 00:02:14.905
A second Vultr call writes the repair. It runs

00:02:14.905 --> 00:02:18.067
on copies in another isolated container, without network access

00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:21.228
or controller credentials. A fresh trusted worker reads the

00:02:21.228 --> 00:02:24.390
output. The controller checks the resulting values independently.
The

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.200
agent cannot mark its own repair as passed.

00:02:27.704 --> 00:02:31.046
One hundred seventeen prices are restored. All one hundred

00:02:31.046 --> 00:02:34.388
twenty-seven invoices remain, including seven newer records,
twenty payment

00:02:34.388 --> 00:02:37.730
updates and three later price edits. The replacements match

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.072
the good version. Every other cell keeps today's value.

00:02:41.584 --> 00:02:44.769
I download the recovered CSV and all one hundred

00:02:44.769 --> 00:02:47.953
seventeen changes. Every data check passed, the originals stayed

00:02:47.953 --> 00:02:51.138
unchanged, and all seven temporary containers were removed before

00:02:51.138 --> 00:02:54.322
download became available. You get a checked copy to

00:02:54.322 --> 00:02:55.384
review and apply.

00:02:55.904 --> 00:02:59.257
This separate containment test also ran on Vultr. A

00:02:59.257 --> 00:03:02.611
program tries to keep running. Rewind stops it at

00:03:02.611 --> 00:03:05.964
the two-second deadline and confirms removal. The broader checks

00:03:05.964 --> 00:03:09.318
also reject whole-backup replacements and unchecked imports. All
fifty-five

00:03:09.318 --> 00:03:10.808
test containers were removed.

00:03:11.308 --> 00:03:14.017
Why would a business pay? Someone has to check

00:03:14.017 --> 00:03:16.726
each supplier price list or finance import, and clean

00:03:16.726 --> 00:03:19.435
up mistakes afterward. Rewind helps that person review routine

00:03:19.435 --> 00:03:22.144
updates and recover precise errors while keeping newer work.

00:03:22.144 --> 00:03:24.853
That is a recurring job with a clear reason

00:03:24.853 --> 00:03:25.756
to come back.
